            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall your house is relatively air tight, however there were a few air leakage locations that require your attention:



** The 2nd floor bathroom fan (replace with an Energy Star unit that operates quieter, uses less electricity &amp; has much less air leakage heat loss through them when not in use)



** The attic access panel (can be perimeter sealed with a removable caulking such as "Draft Attack"



** The bathtub access panel in the closet of the adjoining room.



** The door into the room below the garage (requires weather-stripping &amp; a door sweep)



Savings: With your house already being fairly air tight already, it is tough to achieve a significant reduction. I did model a small 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to potentially save $ 77 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.2 GJ. There are no rebates for air leakage reduction unless you participate in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, which requires the addition of at least R10 insulation to the main floor walls of your house.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 R12. As new housing in the NWT will normally have a minimum R30 walls, I thought I would model the estimated savings for adding 3" (R15) of Extruded Polystyrene Foam Board Insulation to the exterior walls of your house. If you were already planning towards a full exterior siding upgrade on your house, then the insulation rebate &amp; the estimated annual energy savings would be of benefit to you. If you were not already planning an exterior siding upgrade, than this renovation upgrade to your house will have a very long payback.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15 (including the main floor wall that adjoins the garage), you could potentially save $ 605 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

All of the windows on the main &amp; second floor of your house have already been updated to newer triple pane units, so the only 4 older wood windows in your house are in the basement. As an FYI, I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading those 4 older basement windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing the 4 basement windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 27 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.1 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window in rebates through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Pellet Stove Supplemental Heat



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net cost of wood pellets purchased vs. the propane gas saved from your already high efficiency (95.4% AFUE) Rinnai Boiler with integrated HRV Warm Air Furnace) if you were to use the wood pellet stove in the basement for about 75 of your basement heating requirements.



Savings: By using your wood pellet stove for 75% of the heating requirements of your basement, you could potentially save $ 138 per year in energy costs.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - a Lifebreath Clean Air Furnace (incorporates Heat Recovery Ventilation)

        - the kitchen range hood fan

        - 1 bathroom fan

        - the clothes dryer



The 2nd floor bathroom fan has alot of air leakage through it &amp; you may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood fan, bathroom fan and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.4 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current wood pellet stove is not direct-vent (although there is a connection on the back for a dedicated outside air combustion air supply), however it has a power-vented exhaust fan that helps to avoid combustion back-drafting. The Arctic Energy Alliance recommends that all heating &amp; hot water equipment be direct vent, sealed combustion .... whenever possible. Your wood pellet stove is power vented and therefore less susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, there were smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.18 per litre 

Propane - $2.42/m3 community piped system, $0.79 per litre delivered

Wood - $289 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.73 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climate change!gh transportation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
